  "question": {
    "text": "Consider two reactions having same Arrhenius factor A, but different energy of activation.<br />\r(i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20&nbsp;</mn><mi>kJ</mi></math><br />\r(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30&nbsp;</mn><mi>kJ</mi></math><br />\rBoth are at temperature <math><mn>25</mn><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math><br />\rIf temperature in both reaction is increased slightly in such a way that change in temperature in both case is same than choose the correct options."
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "The second reaction is faster",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "The second reaction is more sensitive towards temperature variation",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "If temperature increases, rate of first reaction increase more sharply",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "All the above are correct",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Arrhenius equation;<br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math><br />Here, T and dT same and positive<br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">α</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></msub></math><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">α</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></msub></math><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">α</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></msub></math><br />So, if temperature increases, then rate of reaction having more <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></msub></math> increases more sharply.<br />"
  }
